@meta
id: 32-02
title: Conflict — both sides were right
chapter: 32
type: scenario
skills: conflict-resolution, search-slash, op-linewise
par: 31
minutes: 6
vscode: click "Accept Both Changes", hope it guessed the spacing
open: src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts src/domain/customer/customer.entity.ts src/domain/customer/customer.repository.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
You pulled `main` before opening the MR and got this back:

    Auto-merging src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
    CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in customer.provider.ts
    Automatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result.

Somebody landed `findByEmail` on `main` while you were writing
`deactivate`. Both methods are wanted. Neither touches the other.

Resolve it: both methods stay, `findByEmail` first, one blank line
between them, and no trace of the markers. The file must read as though
the conflict never happened — a stray `=======` gets through review more
often than you would like, and it compiles in some languages.

The entity and the repository are open because you should look at them
before deciding: `active` and `findByEmail` both already exist there.
Neither file changes.

@keys
| `dd` | delete the line under the cursor |
| `cc` `Esc` | replace the line with an empty one |
| `/^<<<` `Enter` | jump to the next marker |
| `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` | delete every marker line in the file |

@hints
A conflict is three lines of vandalism in a file that is otherwise fine.
Deal with the three lines.
--
The `<<<<<<<` line and the `>>>>>>>` line are deletions. The `=======`
line is not — it is sitting exactly where you want a blank line.
--
`/^<<<` `Enter` `dd`, then `/^=` `Enter` `cc` `Esc`, then `/^>>` `Enter`
`dd`. Three searches, three edits, no scrolling.

@solution
`/^<<<` `Enter` `dd` `/^=` `Enter` `cc` `Esc` `/^>>` `Enter` `dd` `:wqa`.
27 keystrokes.

The insight worth keeping is that `=======` is not rubbish to be deleted,
it is a line in the right place with the wrong content. Deleting all
three markers and then opening a blank line back up is one edit more than
changing the middle one in place. On a file with six conflicts that
difference is real.

You could also have written `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d`, which kills all three
markers in one command. It is the right tool when the two sides are being
merged into one block with no gap — a list of imports, a JSON object, a
union type. It is the wrong tool here, because it takes your blank line
with it.

Two habits from real merges:

`/^<<<` is the only search you need to find the next conflict. Anchoring
it at the start of the line means it cannot match a shift operator or a
heredoc inside the code.

Read both sides before you delete either one. Vim makes "accept theirs"
so cheap — `dd` a block, done — that it is tempting to resolve by
picking. Most conflicts are like this one: two people added things next
to each other and both things are wanted. A conflict is a question about
intent, and no tool can answer it for you.

